# p_a4c7 — The Two-Stage Cleanup: Sink at 20, Put Away at 21:30

Hana works an office job in town, out roughly 8:00 to 17:30 on weekdays. Priya is retired and anchors the home, with a morning walk and afternoon errands. The kitchen follows a strict evening ritual: Hana cooks dinner around 18:00 (pan and pot come out of the cupboard to the counter, cutting board stays at the counter, knife comes out of the drawer to the counter). They eat at the kitchen table around 19:00–20:00. Then comes the cleanup, which happens in **two distinct stages** that the existing documents collapse into one.

Stage one (20:00–21:00): dirty dishes, the cutting board, and the knife all go to the **sink** for washing. This is when the robot will find them at sink_k1. Stage two (21:00–22:00): once washed, plates and bowls go to the cupboard, the cutting board goes back to the counter, and the knife goes back to the drawer. By 22:00 the kitchen is reset.

This two-stage model explains the mixed evidence: the cutting board was at sink_k1 at 20:00 (stage one) but at counter_k1 at 22:00 (stage two complete). The pan was at sink_k1 at 20:00 (being washed) but at cupboard_k1 at 22:00 (put away). p_7e4a says things go straight back to their spots (skipping the sink); p_8c3d says they stay at the sink. Both are half-right.

Hana's personal items (laptop, jacket, keys, handbag, wallet, sunglasses, shoes, scarf, water bottle, phone, charger, pen) are all out of the house during her work hours. The guitar sits on bedroom_floor_b1 at all times; no one plays it during the observation window. Priya's keys, jacket, and wallet stay at the entry during her home hours but leave for her afternoon errands.

What would refute this document: if the cutting board or knife is found at the counter or drawer during the 20:00–21:00 window (before the sink stage), or if plates are still at the sink after 22:00 (cleanup not finished).

